WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.477
Idea. A business keeps money in two places: the till and the bank. A cash book records both, with a cash column and a bank column. Money in goes on the debit side, money out on the credit side.

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:26.613
A three-column cash book adds a discount column on each side. Discount allowed sits on the debit side, discount received on the credit side. These columns are only totalled, never balanced.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:38.995
An overdraft is a credit balance. Cash paid into the bank is a contra, entered on both sides. A dishonoured cheque, standing orders and direct debits take money out of the bank.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:50.351
Method. First, the opening balances. Cash goes on the debit side. Bank goes on the debit side if there is money in it, the credit side if overdrawn.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:01:03.727
Next, enter receipts on the debit side and payments on the credit side, naming the other account in the details. With cash discount, enter the money actually paid, and the discount on the same line.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:13.409
Finally, total the discount columns, and balance cash and bank separately: carry down on the last day, bring down on the first day of next month.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:27.933
Worked example. Here is a real question from November 2025. Kate keeps a three-column cash book. Enter her August transactions, balance it, and bring the balances down. Ten marks.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:37.597
Kate starts with cash of one hundred and twelve dollars, on the debit side, and a bank overdraft of four hundred and twenty-five, on the credit side.

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:52.996
On August the second, Kate pays R O H four hundred and ninety-four after five per cent discount. That is ninety-five per cent of the invoice, so the invoice was five hundred and twenty: discount received, twenty-six.

00:01:53.550 --> 00:02:08.791
On the debit side: cash sales of eight hundred and thirty-five, and the contra, seven hundred and ninety-five into the bank. Alzate pays nine hundred and eighty less five per cent: discount forty-nine, bank nine hundred and thirty-one.

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:23.976
On the credit side: the overdraft, R O H, and Slater's dishonoured cheque, three hundred and twenty, taken back out of the bank. Then the contra in cash, the standing order of two hundred and twenty, and the cleaner, thirty-five.

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:42.245
Balance each column separately. Cash: nine hundred and forty-seven in, eight hundred and thirty out, leaving one hundred and seventeen. Bank: one thousand seven hundred and twenty-six in, one thousand four hundred and fifty-nine out, leaving two hundred and sixty-seven.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:50.339
Both balances are carried down on the credit side, the smaller side, so each column totals the same on both sides.

00:02:50.900 --> 00:03:05.011
Both balances come down on the debit side on the first of September: cash one hundred and seventeen, bank two hundred and sixty-seven. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:14.404
Common mistake. A common mistake is putting an opening overdraft on the debit side. It is owed to the bank, so it is a credit.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.363
Another is entering a contra on one side only. It always appears on both sides.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:29.608
Your turn. Now you try. Enter Omar's March transactions, then balance his cash book. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:48.946
Answer. Delta: one hundred and ninety is ninety-five per cent, so the invoice was two hundred and the discount ten. Cash: five hundred in, four hundred and seventy out, leaving thirty. Bank: six hundred and fifty in, one hundred and ninety out, leaving four hundred and sixty.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:04:00.050
On the credit side: the contra, Delta with ten discount, the wages, and both balances carried down. Bring them down on the debit side on the first of April.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:10.295
Remember: opening balances, each line in its column, then total the discounts and balance cash and bank separately. Now try the practice questions.
